  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A run's daemon dies during a cleanup and the run doesn't run another command for a long time, for example while it waits on a question. Meanwhile process IDs wrap around, an unrelated program owned by the same user takes the old group or session ID, and that program's leader exits while its children keep running. When the run resumes, recovery treats those children as the run's own processes and force-kills the owner's unrelated work.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: this needs the macOS kernel to reuse one specific process-group or session ID, plus a leader that exits while its members survive. That can't be forced from a test here. The code is quoted instead.",
   "evidence": "At head, recovery (src/util/proc.ts:146-154) passes the saved `context.leader` straight to `markedProcesses`. Discovery then accepts numeric session/group membership when `birth === leader.birth || birth === null` (src/util/processes-darwin.ts:330-333, comment: 'An exited leader's observed session/group remains ours'). That rule holds during live cleanup, but not across downtime. Recovery only runs lazily, before the run's next command (proc.ts:237-238), so the gap can be long. The `predatesSpawn` filter doesn't help: processes started during the downtime come after `spawnedAt`. Each newly found pid gets its current birth recorded (proc.ts:160-161), so the later birth check is circular and the pid is SIGKILLed. At base, recovery ran no discovery and only killed recorded pids whose birth matched, so this path is new."
