   },
   "evidence": "The throwaway probe constructed stale persisted context and started an actual detached Darwin group whose leader exited, leaving an unmarked child; it simulated the post-reuse state rather than forcing kernel ID reuse. Native discovery excluded the child without the saved leader but included it with that leader, and actual HEAD recovery killed it. HEAD passes `context.leader` directly into discovery (proc.ts:144\u2013151); processes-darwin.ts:334 accepts membership when `birth === leader.birth || birth === null`. Confirming the newly discovered child\u2019s birth proves its identity, not ownership. The helper behavior existed at base, but base recovery only inspected recorded processes and did not expose it to persisted group IDs across downtime. Running the same probe with temporary base and later source copies produced no signal to the unrelated child."
